Reason:
The first statement copies an untrusted user-supplied `cpu_id` without any validation against the system's CPU count. The second statement passes this uncontrolled value directly to `cpumask_test_cpu()`, which expands to a `test_bit()` that indexes the cpumask bitmap using `cpu_id / BITS_PER_LONG`. Without a prior bound check, this can cause an out-of-bounds read of the bitmap and, on kernels with `CONFIG_DEBUG_PER_CPU_MAPS`, trigger a `WARN_ON_ONCE()` that may panic the system.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (dmah->cpu_id >= nr_cpu_ids) {
	ret = -EINVAL;
	goto err;
}
Reason:
This added check verifies that the user-supplied `cpu_id` is less than `nr_cpu_ids` (the number of possible CPUs) before it is used in `cpumask_test_cpu()`. By rejecting out-of-range values with `-EINVAL`, it prevents the out-of-bounds access and avoids the potential system crash, ensuring that only valid CPU IDs are passed to the cpumask operation.

